1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,629
This is ECE 358, video 7. The topic is the transport layer, multiplexing, and UDP. It covers Chapter 3, slides 1 to 24.

2
00:00:13,079 --> 00:00:17,474
After this video, you can explain the services of the transport layer.

3
00:00:17,924 --> 00:00:23,513
You can find the socket that receives a segment, for UDP and for TCP.

4
00:00:23,963 --> 00:00:27,547
You can explain UDP, its header, and its checksum.

5
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:33,843
And you can answer questions 1 to 18 of the transport section of the question bank.

6
00:00:34,293 --> 00:00:40,309
Start with the problem. The network layer, IP, delivers a datagram to a host.

7
00:00:40,759 --> 00:00:45,089
But many processes run on that host. The data is for one of them.

8
00:00:45,539 --> 00:00:50,019
Look at the real path of the data. It goes down the stack of host A.

9
00:00:50,469 --> 00:00:52,923
Then it goes across the links and the router.

10
00:00:53,373 --> 00:00:55,570
Then it goes up the stack of host B.

11
00:00:56,020 --> 00:01:03,081
The transport layer hides this path. It gives logical communication between processes on different hosts.

12
00:01:03,531 --> 00:01:14,262
The slides call this inter-process communication, or IPC. The processes can be client and server processes, or peer-to-peer processes.

13
00:01:14,712 --> 00:01:20,621
The router has only the network, link and physical layers. It has no transport layer.

14
00:01:21,071 --> 00:01:25,978
So only the end systems, host A and host B, run the transport layer.

15
00:01:26,428 --> 00:01:33,297
What does the transport layer do with the data? At the sender, it is passed an application-layer message.

16
00:01:33,747 --> 00:01:37,715
It sets the values of the header fields. The header is T h.

17
00:01:38,165 --> 00:01:44,693
Then it creates a segment. A segment is the transport header, T h, plus the application message.

18
00:01:45,143 --> 00:01:48,599
It passes the segment to IP, the network layer.

19
00:01:49,049 --> 00:01:53,508
At the receiver, the transport layer receives the segment from IP.

20
00:01:53,958 --> 00:01:55,707
It checks the header values.

21
00:01:56,157 --> 00:01:58,909
It extracts the application-layer message.

22
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:05,034
Then it demultiplexes the message up to the correct process, P 1, through its socket.

23
00:02:05,484 --> 00:02:12,140
A large message can be cut into many segments, here 3. The receiver puts them together again.

24
00:02:12,590 --> 00:02:24,259
The slides list four services of the transport layer. The first is multiplexing and demultiplexing. On a host, many applications send data at the same time.

25
00:02:24,709 --> 00:02:30,789
Their streams must share one stream at the L4, L3 interface, to the network layer.

26
00:02:31,239 --> 00:02:46,279
The second service is reliable data transfer. Between a client and a server, the data must arrive with no bit error, no out-of-order delivery, no loss, and no duplicate. Remember the letters B, O, L, D.

27
00:02:46,729 --> 00:02:52,660
The third service is flow control. A fast server must not overwhelm a slow client.

28
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:57,185
The client tells the server if it is willing to receive data now.

29
00:02:57,635 --> 00:03:00,451
It decides from the free space in its buffer.

30
00:03:00,901 --> 00:03:04,143
So the receiving process performs flow control.

31
00:03:04,593 --> 00:03:09,500
The fourth service is congestion control. Routers have finite buffers.

32
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:16,713
If apps send too fast, the buffers fill, and the routers drop packets. The network is congested.

33
00:03:17,163 --> 00:03:22,645
TCP has congestion control. The sender of the data stream performs it.

34
00:03:23,095 --> 00:03:39,031
The Internet gives applications two transport protocols. The first is TCP, the Transmission Control Protocol. TCP exchanges control information before the data, for example a request and an ack.

35
00:03:39,481 --> 00:03:42,169
So TCP is connection-oriented.

36
00:03:42,619 --> 00:03:51,857
The second is UDP, the User Datagram Protocol. UDP sends the data at once, with no control messages first.

37
00:03:52,307 --> 00:03:54,376
So UDP is connectionless.

38
00:03:54,826 --> 00:04:01,951
TCP gives connection setup, reliable in-order delivery, flow control, and congestion control.

39
00:04:02,401 --> 00:04:08,353
UDP gives none of these four. It is a no-frills extension of best-effort IP.

40
00:04:08,803 --> 00:04:15,481
Two services are not available from either protocol: delay guarantees and bandwidth guarantees.

41
00:04:15,931 --> 00:04:20,859
Both protocols run over best-effort IP, so neither can promise them.

42
00:04:21,309 --> 00:04:26,749
Look at one host with three client processes: App 1, App 2 and App 3.

43
00:04:27,199 --> 00:04:36,991
Each process has a socket, the yellow box on the L5, L4 line. A socket is the interface between layer 5 and layer 4.

44
00:04:37,441 --> 00:04:44,822
The three apps send data at the same time. So there are three data streams at the L5, L4 interface.

45
00:04:45,272 --> 00:04:53,187
The transport layer combines them into one data stream at the L4, L3 interface. This is multiplexing.

46
00:04:53,637 --> 00:04:58,543
Now data arrives at a host. One stream comes up from the network layer.

47
00:04:58,993 --> 00:05:05,585
The transport layer splits it into three streams, one for each socket. This is demultiplexing.

48
00:05:06,035 --> 00:05:14,377
In general, for N pairs of client and server processes, there are N sockets for the clients and N sockets for the servers.

49
00:05:14,827 --> 00:05:20,480
Here is slide 13. A server host runs two processes, P1 and P2.

50
00:05:20,930 --> 00:05:24,749
Two client hosts run the processes P3 and P4.

51
00:05:25,199 --> 00:05:31,620
P3 talks to P1. Its segments go down, across the network, and up into the server.

52
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:36,891
P4 talks to P2. Its segments also go up into the server.

53
00:05:37,341 --> 00:05:42,376
Below the transport layer of the server, both conversations are one stream.

54
00:05:42,826 --> 00:05:49,482
At a sender, multiplexing takes data from many sockets. It adds a transport header to each segment.

55
00:05:49,932 --> 00:05:59,553
At a receiver, demultiplexing uses the header information. It delivers each segment to the correct socket, here P1 or P2.

56
00:06:00,003 --> 00:06:10,627
How does a host know the correct socket? Start with the IP datagram. Each datagram has a source IP address and a destination IP address.

57
00:06:11,077 --> 00:06:14,469
Each datagram carries one transport-layer segment.

58
00:06:14,919 --> 00:06:19,229
Each segment has a source port number and a destination port number.

59
00:06:19,679 --> 00:06:23,369
Then come the other header fields and the application data.

60
00:06:23,819 --> 00:06:30,390
The host uses the IP addresses and the port numbers to direct the segment to the correct socket.

61
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:45,176
Port numbers are 16-bit numbers. So there are 2 to the power 16, which is 65536 port numbers, from 0 to 65535.

62
00:06:45,626 --> 00:06:50,277
There are two kinds. Reserved ports are a few thousand at the lower end.

63
00:06:50,727 --> 00:06:52,497
Free ports are the rest.

64
00:06:52,947 --> 00:06:57,342
Standard server apps use the reserved ports. They are well known.

65
00:06:57,792 --> 00:07:07,115
For TCP: 80 for HTTP, 179 for BGP, and 20 and 21 for FTP.

66
00:07:07,565 --> 00:07:17,869
The slide lists 520 for RIP and 25 for SMTP under UDP. SMTP normally uses TCP.

67
00:07:18,319 --> 00:07:26,276
For your own app, use a free port. In Python, bind a socket with port 0, and the OS assigns a free port.

68
00:07:26,726 --> 00:07:37,094
Now UDP demultiplexing. When a process creates a socket, the socket gets a host-local port number. The OS assigns a free port.

69
00:07:37,544 --> 00:07:43,112
When the process sends a message into the UDP socket, it must give two values.

70
00:07:43,562 --> 00:07:49,770
The first is the destination IP address. The process gets it from a DNS server.

71
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:56,812
The second is the destination port number. For well-known client-server apps, this number is known.

72
00:07:57,262 --> 00:08:06,969
When the receiving host gets a UDP segment, it checks the destination port number in the segment, here 6428.

73
00:08:07,419 --> 00:08:14,971
Then it directs the segment to the socket with that port number, UDP socket 6428.

74
00:08:15,421 --> 00:08:19,858
Segments with the same destination port number go to the same socket.

75
00:08:20,308 --> 00:08:25,364
This is true for different source IP addresses and different source port numbers.

76
00:08:25,814 --> 00:08:34,049
Here is the example of slide 16. The server socket uses UDP port 6428.

77
00:08:34,499 --> 00:08:40,493
One client socket, my socket 2, uses port 9157.

78
00:08:40,943 --> 00:08:47,237
The other client socket, my socket 1, uses port 5775.

79
00:08:47,687 --> 00:08:56,092
P3 sends a request to the server. The source port is 9157, the port of the sending socket.

80
00:08:56,542 --> 00:09:02,366
The destination port is 6428, the port of the server socket.

81
00:09:02,816 --> 00:09:14,379
The server replies. It swaps the two ports. The source port is 6428, and the destination port is 9157.

82
00:09:14,829 --> 00:09:22,807
Now P4 talks to the server. The slide has question marks for these two segments. Pause the video and fill them in.

83
00:09:23,257 --> 00:09:33,817
P4 sends a request. The source port is 5775, and the destination port is 6428.

84
00:09:34,267 --> 00:09:43,782
The reply to P4 has source port 6428 and destination port 5775.

85
00:09:44,232 --> 00:09:49,011
Both requests carry destination port 6428.

86
00:09:49,461 --> 00:09:53,919
So the server host delivers both of them to the same socket, P1.

87
00:09:54,369 --> 00:10:12,353
TCP is different. A TCP socket is identified by a 4-tuple: source IP address, source port number, destination IP address, and destination port number. The 4-tuple is also called the TCP connection ID.

88
00:10:12,803 --> 00:10:18,841
Here, server B uses port 80. It has three sockets, P4, P5 and P6.

89
00:10:19,291 --> 00:10:21,488
Host A and host C are clients.

90
00:10:21,938 --> 00:10:30,215
Process P1 on host A sends a segment from A, port 9157, to B, port 80.

91
00:10:30,665 --> 00:10:35,828
The server uses all four values. The segment goes to socket P4.

92
00:10:36,278 --> 00:10:43,702
P2 on host C sends from C, port 5775, to B, port 80.

93
00:10:44,152 --> 00:10:46,925
That 4-tuple belongs to socket P6.

94
00:10:47,375 --> 00:10:54,565
P3 on host C sends from C, port 9157, to B, port 80.

95
00:10:55,015 --> 00:10:59,644
Host A also used source port 9157.

96
00:11:00,094 --> 00:11:03,614
But the source IP addresses are different: A and C.

97
00:11:04,064 --> 00:11:07,221
So the segment goes to a third socket, P5.

98
00:11:07,671 --> 00:11:11,383
All three segments go to IP address B, port 80.

99
00:11:11,833 --> 00:11:17,337
They reach three different sockets. Each socket belongs to a different connecting client.

100
00:11:17,787 --> 00:11:26,043
UDP would look only at port 80 and use one socket for all three. TCP reads all four values.

101
00:11:26,493 --> 00:11:36,115
Here is the summary. Demultiplexing uses header field values. The datagram header has the source and the destination IP addresses.

102
00:11:36,565 --> 00:11:40,767
The segment header has the source and the destination port numbers.

103
00:11:41,217 --> 00:11:48,556
UDP demultiplexes with the destination port number only. Many sources can share one socket.

104
00:11:49,006 --> 00:11:55,513
TCP demultiplexes with the 4-tuple: both IP addresses and both port numbers.

105
00:11:55,963 --> 00:11:58,565
So each connection has its own socket.

106
00:11:59,015 --> 00:12:04,989
Multiplexing and demultiplexing also happen at the other layers, with different parameters.

107
00:12:05,439 --> 00:12:18,281
Now UDP itself. UDP is a no-frills, bare-bones protocol. It adds a UDP header to the app data and passes the segment to IP, the network layer.

108
00:12:18,731 --> 00:12:23,979
At the receiver, UDP removes the header and gives the app data to the app.

109
00:12:24,429 --> 00:12:29,165
There is no handshaking. The sender sends S1 at once, and it arrives.

110
00:12:29,615 --> 00:12:37,743
UDP gives best-effort service. A segment can be lost. Here S2 is lost. UDP never resends it.

111
00:12:38,193 --> 00:12:43,868
Each segment is handled on its own. Here S3 takes a longer time than S4.

112
00:12:44,318 --> 00:12:46,366
So S4 arrives first.

113
00:12:46,816 --> 00:12:53,493
The app receives S1, S4, S3. S2 is lost, and S4 comes before S3.

114
00:12:53,943 --> 00:13:01,197
A UDP segment has no sequence number. So the receiver cannot find the gap or restore the order.

115
00:13:01,647 --> 00:13:04,847
The slides call this: send and hope for the best.

116
00:13:05,297 --> 00:13:16,070
Why use UDP? Look at a short request and reply. If the protocol sets up a connection first, the control messages take one round-trip time.

117
00:13:16,520 --> 00:13:25,501
Then the request and the reply take one more RTT. So the reply comes after 1 plus 1, which is 2 RTTs.

118
00:13:25,951 --> 00:13:32,714
With UDP, there is no connection establishment. The reply comes after 1 RTT.

119
00:13:33,164 --> 00:13:40,481
This is the first reason for UDP: no connection establishment, so no added RTT delay.

120
00:13:40,931 --> 00:13:46,563
Second, UDP is simple. There is no connection state at the sender or the receiver.

121
00:13:47,013 --> 00:13:50,320
Third, the header is small. It is only 8 bytes.

122
00:13:50,770 --> 00:13:56,530
Fourth, there is no congestion control. UDP can send as fast as the app wants.

123
00:13:56,980 --> 00:13:59,668
So it can function in the face of congestion.

124
00:14:00,118 --> 00:14:04,257
Who uses UDP? First, streaming multimedia apps.

125
00:14:04,707 --> 00:14:08,077
They are loss tolerant, and they are rate sensitive.

126
00:14:08,527 --> 00:14:10,767
DNS uses UDP.

127
00:14:11,217 --> 00:14:17,425
SNMP, the Simple Network Management Protocol, also uses UDP.

128
00:14:17,875 --> 00:14:23,849
HTTP 3 uses QUIC, the Quick UDP Internet Connection protocol.

129
00:14:24,299 --> 00:14:32,768
QUIC is TCP functions, plus transport layer security, plus multiplexing, redesigned over UDP.

130
00:14:33,218 --> 00:14:39,511
With HTTP 3 there is no head-of-line blocking, as there is with TCP.

131
00:14:39,961 --> 00:14:47,428
So, if an app needs reliable transfer over UDP, it adds the reliability at the application layer.

132
00:14:47,878 --> 00:14:54,385
It also adds congestion control at the application layer. HTTP 3 does this.

133
00:14:54,835 --> 00:14:59,571
Here is the UDP segment format. Each row is 32 bits.

134
00:15:00,021 --> 00:15:09,215
The first row has the source port number and the destination port number, 16 bits each. They name the sending and the receiving socket.

135
00:15:09,665 --> 00:15:13,057
Under the header is the application data, the payload.

136
00:15:13,507 --> 00:15:17,070
The second row starts with the length field, 16 bits.

137
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:22,811
Length is the number of bytes in the whole UDP segment, including the header.

138
00:15:23,261 --> 00:15:31,303
The header is 2 rows of 32 bits. That is 64 bits, and 64 divided by 8 is 8 bytes.

139
00:15:31,753 --> 00:15:39,967
For example, 120 bytes of data give a length of 120 plus 8, which is 128.

140
00:15:40,417 --> 00:15:47,883
The last field is the checksum, 16 bits. It is used for error detection, that is, to find flipped bits.

141
00:15:48,333 --> 00:16:00,920
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. It shows how the checksum finds flipped bits. The sender treats the segment as 16-bit words. Here are word 1 and word 2.

142
00:16:01,370 --> 00:16:05,466
Add them. The sum fits in 16 bits. There is no carry.

143
00:16:05,916 --> 00:16:07,601
Now add word 3.

144
00:16:08,051 --> 00:16:10,889
This time there is a carry out of bit 15.

145
00:16:11,339 --> 00:16:17,995
The carry goes back into bit 0: add 1 to the 16-bit sum. This is called wraparound.

146
00:16:18,445 --> 00:16:24,034
Then invert every bit. The result is the checksum. The sender puts it in the header.

147
00:16:24,484 --> 00:16:30,777
The receiver adds all the words and the checksum. The sum of the words is the same as at the sender.

148
00:16:31,227 --> 00:16:37,926
Each bit of the checksum is the inverse of the bit above it. So with no error, the result is all ones.

149
00:16:38,376 --> 00:16:45,224
Now the last bit of word 2 flips. The sum of the words is 1 less. The result ends in a 0.

150
00:16:45,674 --> 00:16:51,413
A 0 bit means an error. The receiver detects the error and discards the segment.

151
00:16:51,863 --> 00:16:57,751
Now the question bank. Here are questions 1, 2 and 4. Pause the video and try them.

152
00:16:58,201 --> 00:17:05,305
Question 1. The main function is inter-process communication, IPC, between two computers.

153
00:17:05,755 --> 00:17:11,408
That is, logical communication between application processes on different hosts.

154
00:17:11,858 --> 00:17:16,786
Question 2. The two main protocols are TCP and UDP.

155
00:17:17,236 --> 00:17:21,119
Question 4. Delay and bandwidth are not guaranteed.

156
00:17:21,569 --> 00:17:26,710
Both protocols run over best-effort IP. So there are no guarantees.

157
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:34,008
Question 3 asks for a table that compares TCP and UDP. Pause the video and try it.

158
00:17:34,458 --> 00:17:38,789
TCP is connection-oriented. UDP is connectionless.

159
00:17:39,239 --> 00:17:43,996
TCP gives reliable data transfer. UDP does not.

160
00:17:44,446 --> 00:17:48,499
TCP gives in-order delivery. UDP does not.

161
00:17:48,949 --> 00:17:52,768
TCP has flow control. UDP has none.

162
00:17:53,218 --> 00:18:00,855
TCP has congestion control. UDP has none. The slides say UDP can blast away.

163
00:18:01,305 --> 00:18:06,297
TCP keeps connection state at both ends. UDP keeps none.

164
00:18:06,747 --> 00:18:11,505
TCP uses sequence numbers. A UDP segment has none.

165
00:18:11,955 --> 00:18:17,181
The TCP header is larger. The UDP header is only 8 bytes.

166
00:18:17,631 --> 00:18:21,429
Neither protocol gives a delay or a bandwidth guarantee.

167
00:18:21,879 --> 00:18:25,484
Questions 5 and 6. Pause the video and try them.

168
00:18:25,934 --> 00:18:33,059
Question 5. Multiplexing combines the N app data streams at the L5, L4 interface.

169
00:18:33,509 --> 00:18:37,989
It makes one network-layer stream at the L4, L3 interface.

170
00:18:38,439 --> 00:18:44,199
Demultiplexing does the reverse. It splits that one stream back into N app streams.

171
00:18:44,649 --> 00:18:49,855
For N client-server pairs, there are N client sockets and N server sockets.

172
00:18:50,305 --> 00:18:54,102
With 3 pairs, that is 3 plus 3, which is 6 sockets.

173
00:18:54,552 --> 00:18:59,608
Question 6. A socket is the L5, L4 interface on a host.

174
00:19:00,058 --> 00:19:03,365
A process sends and receives data through its socket.

175
00:19:03,815 --> 00:19:08,508
The transport layer finds the socket from the port numbers in the segment.

176
00:19:08,958 --> 00:19:13,161
Questions 7, 8 and 9. Pause the video and try them.

177
00:19:13,611 --> 00:19:21,141
Question 7. A UDP socket is identified by its destination port number, the host-local port.

178
00:19:21,591 --> 00:19:25,986
The destination IP address brings the datagram to the host.

179
00:19:26,436 --> 00:19:29,487
Then the host checks only the destination port.

180
00:19:29,937 --> 00:19:41,499
Question 8. A TCP socket is identified by the 4-tuple: source IP address, source port, destination IP address, and destination port.

181
00:19:41,949 --> 00:19:48,925
Question 9. UDP uses only the destination port, so many clients share one socket.

182
00:19:49,375 --> 00:19:54,815
TCP uses the 4-tuple, so each connecting client has its own socket.

183
00:19:55,265 --> 00:20:07,767
In the example of slide 18, A port 9157 and C port 9157 reach different sockets, P4 and P5.

184
00:20:08,217 --> 00:20:12,867
Questions 10, 11 and 14. Pause the video and try them.

185
00:20:13,317 --> 00:20:22,768
Question 10, the positive aspects of UDP. There is no connection establishment, so no RTT delay before the data.

186
00:20:23,218 --> 00:20:25,906
It is simple, with no connection state.

187
00:20:26,356 --> 00:20:28,233
The header is only 8 bytes.

188
00:20:28,683 --> 00:20:33,313
There is no congestion control, so it sends as fast as the app wants.

189
00:20:33,763 --> 00:20:35,981
And the checksum detects flipped bits.

190
00:20:36,431 --> 00:20:45,157
Question 11, the negative aspects. UDP is best effort. Segments may be lost, and UDP never resends them.

191
00:20:45,607 --> 00:20:50,620
Segments may arrive out of order, and there are no sequence numbers to fix this.

192
00:20:51,070 --> 00:20:52,947
There is no flow control.

193
00:20:53,397 --> 00:20:59,008
Question 14. Some apps need speed and a steady rate more than reliability.

194
00:20:59,458 --> 00:21:02,551
Streaming is loss tolerant and rate sensitive.

195
00:21:03,001 --> 00:21:10,041
If an app needs reliability, it adds it at the application layer, as HTTP 3 does.

196
00:21:10,491 --> 00:21:16,486
Questions 12 and 13 ask the same about TCP. Pause the video and try them.

197
00:21:16,936 --> 00:21:23,101
Question 12, the positive aspects. TCP gives reliable, in-order delivery.

198
00:21:23,551 --> 00:21:27,157
It has flow control, which protects the receive buffer.

199
00:21:27,607 --> 00:21:31,660
It has congestion control, which protects the router buffers.

200
00:21:32,110 --> 00:21:39,257
Question 13, the negative aspects. Connection setup adds RTT delay before the data.

201
00:21:39,707 --> 00:21:41,989
Both ends must keep connection state.

202
00:21:42,439 --> 00:21:46,194
Also, congestion control can lower the sending rate.

203
00:21:46,644 --> 00:21:51,700
And there is head-of-line blocking. Here the sender sends segments 1 to 4.

204
00:21:52,150 --> 00:21:55,819
Segment 2 is lost. Segments 3 and 4 arrive.

205
00:21:56,269 --> 00:22:03,352
But TCP gives the data to the app in order only. So 3 and 4 wait until 2 is resent.

206
00:22:03,802 --> 00:22:06,874
HTTP 3 over QUIC avoids this.

207
00:22:07,324 --> 00:22:11,313
Questions 15 and 16. Pause the video and try them.

208
00:22:11,763 --> 00:22:16,734
Protocols that use UDP. First, DNS, for name lookup.

209
00:22:17,184 --> 00:22:20,171
SNMP, for network management.

210
00:22:20,621 --> 00:22:22,903
HTTP 3, through QUIC.

211
00:22:23,353 --> 00:22:27,343
The slides also list RIP, on port 520.

212
00:22:27,793 --> 00:22:31,057
Streaming multimedia apps use UDP too.

213
00:22:31,507 --> 00:22:36,776
Protocols that use TCP. First, HTTP, on port 80.

214
00:22:37,226 --> 00:22:40,447
FTP, on ports 20 and 21.

215
00:22:40,897 --> 00:22:44,481
And BGP, on port 179.

216
00:22:44,931 --> 00:22:55,150
The slide lists SMTP under UDP. SMTP normally uses TCP, so do not use it as a UDP example.

217
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:59,653
Questions 17 and 18. Pause the video and try them.

218
00:23:00,103 --> 00:23:11,623
Question 17. The first row of the UDP header has the source port and the destination port, 16 bits each. They name the sending and the receiving socket.

219
00:23:12,073 --> 00:23:17,641
Then comes the length, 16 bits. It counts the bytes of the segment, with the header.

220
00:23:18,091 --> 00:23:24,726
Then comes the checksum, 16 bits, for error detection. The application data follows the header.

221
00:23:25,176 --> 00:23:30,680
So the header is 4 times 16, which is 64 bits. That is 8 bytes.

222
00:23:31,130 --> 00:23:34,586
Question 18. The length field has 16 bits.

223
00:23:35,036 --> 00:23:43,612
Its largest value is 2 to the power 16, minus 1. That is 65535 bytes.

224
00:23:44,062 --> 00:23:52,915
The length counts the whole segment, header included. So the whole segment is at most 65535 bytes.

225
00:23:53,365 --> 00:23:55,349
Subtract the 8-byte header.

226
00:23:55,799 --> 00:24:09,325
The maximum data is 65535 minus 8, which is 65527 bytes. This is the answer from the UDP header alone.

227
00:24:09,775 --> 00:24:27,481
Optional, and not on the slides: an IP version 4 datagram also has a header of at least 20 bytes. So the real limit is 65535 minus 8 minus 20, which is 65507 bytes.

228
00:24:27,931 --> 00:24:36,849
Here is the recap. One. The transport layer gives logical communication between processes. Only the end systems run it.

229
00:24:37,299 --> 00:24:48,307
Two. Its services are multiplexing and demultiplexing, reliable data transfer, flow control by the receiver, and congestion control by the sender.

230
00:24:48,757 --> 00:24:59,061
Three. TCP is connection-oriented and reliable. UDP is connectionless and best effort. Neither guarantees delay or bandwidth.

231
00:24:59,511 --> 00:25:11,948
Four. Port numbers have 16 bits, from 0 to 65535. Know the well-known ports. Port 0 asks the OS for a free port.

232
00:25:12,398 --> 00:25:19,801
Five. UDP demultiplexes by the destination port only. TCP uses the 4-tuple.

233
00:25:20,251 --> 00:25:26,821
Six. UDP has no setup, no state, an 8-byte header, and no congestion control.

234
00:25:27,271 --> 00:25:50,738
Seven. The UDP header has source port, destination port, length, and checksum. The maximum data is 65535 minus 8, which is 65527 bytes. The next video starts reliable data transfer: stop-and-wait and Go-Back-N. That is the end of video 7.
